# Overview

Wyrd is the registry and observation layer for AI systems. It connects
data, models, prompts, agents, and the observations they generate under
one typed contract.

## The coordination problem

An AI system is not one artifact. It is a dataset, the model trained on
it, the prompt that calls the model, the agent that runs the prompt, and
the workflow that orchestrates the agents. In practice, these live in
separate systems: a model registry here, a prompt file in a repo there,
a feature store, a YAML config, a notebook. Each tool has its own format
and its own ID scheme. Nothing types the boundaries between them.

The result: you cannot answer basic questions about your own system.

- Which dataset trained the model this prompt calls?
- If I change this prompt, what agents depend on it?
- Is the prompt running in production the one that was reviewed?

You have a model registry, an experiment tracker, an LLM tracing tool.
None of them know about each other. None of them can tell you whether the
model you're tracing is the one your prompt registered, or whether the data
that trained it is the version that passed your eval suite.

There is a second problem running in parallel. Most teams are already
collecting data that would answer harder questions: commits, agent traces,
evals, logs, metrics, drift signals. That data exists. It sits in systems
that have no concept of the components that generated it. When a model
starts drifting, you cannot tell from the metrics alone which data trained
it, which prompts call it, or which agent workflows will be affected. AI
system behavior is a function of how components communicate and fail, not
just what they are.

Every team rebuilds this connective tissue by hand with naming conventions,
wiki pages, and tribal knowledge, and it rots. Wyrd's premise is that the
connective tissue should be typed and derived, not documented, and that
observations should connect to that structure rather than sit in a separate
silo you only query when something breaks.

## How Wyrd answers it

You describe every component in your AI system as a card. Wyrd records
them, validates them, links them, and connects the observations they generate.
The loop is four verbs:

1. **Author** a card in Python: a `DataCard`, `ModelCard`, or `PromptCard`.
2. **Register** it: the server validates the spec and stores it under an
   immutable `(space, name, version)` identity. Registration is the HTTP API
   today; the Python package authors cards locally.
3. **Refer** to other cards with a `CardRef`. The server derives the lineage
   graph from those refs. You never author edges directly.
4. **Run**: Skald resolves cards from the registry at execution time. The
   prompt you registered is the prompt that runs.

Observations (traces, evals, metrics, drift signals) attach to the cards
that generated them. When something breaks in production, you have a typed
path from the alert back to the prompt, the model, and the dataset that
produced it.

Every card, of every kind, shares one envelope: `apiVersion`, `metadata`,
`kind`, `spec`, `relationships`, `status`. The `kind` selects the spec
schema; everything else is constant. That single shape makes the whole
system inspectable from one contract. See [Card](/concepts/card/) for the
envelope in detail.

## What runs today

<Aside title="Three kinds run end-to-end" data-variant="wyrd">

**Data**, **Model**, and **Prompt** are fully implemented: authored in
Python, validated, registered, referenced, and resolvable at runtime.
**Skald** runs agents and workflows over registered prompts today. You can
register a full inference pipeline, trace it, and run agents against it.

The other thirteen kinds plus `External` are reserved structure: their
specs and schemas are authored, but there is no runtime behind them yet.
Every page that references a reserved kind says so. See [Cards](/cards/)
for per-kind status.

</Aside>

